UnityPy 1.25.2 (with UnityPyBoost, its C++ extension)
https://github.com/K0lb3/UnityPy
Licence: MIT.

unityrs-core ports the read and write rules of UnityPy 1.25.2 and of the
UnityPyBoost C++ extension shipped in the same release, and unityrs-tpk
ports UnityPy 1.25.2's UnityPy/helpers/Tpk.py (how a type-tree package is
read and a class's type tree picked from it). The dump (unityrs-dump),
which loads its inputs by UnityPy's rules, the test kit (unityrs-testkit),
which writes inputs in the layouts UnityPy reads, and the P0 LZMA spike
(unityrs-bench-lzma), which finds a bundle's blocks by BundleFile.read_fs's
rules, follow UnityPy too. A file that ports UnityPy code names its UnityPy
source in its header ("Ported from UnityPy 1.25.2 (MIT): ..."), and a file
holding tables tools/gen/gen_tables.py generates from UnityPy's source says
so in its own ("Generated by tools/gen/gen_tables.py from UnityPy 1.25.2
(MIT): ..."). The tables in crates/unityrs-core/src/classid.rs and
crates/unityrs-core/src/typetree/commonstring.rs are generated that way,
from UnityPy/enums/ClassIDType.py and UnityPy/enums/CommonString.py.
scripts/unitypy_citations.py checks every citation of a line of UnityPy
1.25.2 or UnityPyBoost in the repository (code, tools, scripts and
documents) written as path:line, path:first-last or a bare :line after its
file is named, the forms its docstring lists (it refuses a line cited in
words beside a UnityPy file's name, and reads no other wording): each cited
line exists, a cited range is a whole span of statements that cuts no
function, each path written as UnityPy's names one of its files, and each
file under a crate's src/ that cites one (test modules aside) has one of
those headers.

